      "name": "Question 8",
      "problem": "Find the equation of the horizontal asymptote of the function $f(x)={(\\frac{1}{2})}^{x-3}+4.$",
      "worked_solution": "Let's plot the graph of $y=f(x):$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/linear/14254086/assets/47d570c5b05096a0e8b7a13df510d8cd71dbee93dd2bf58dae18d7ff49ff1787.png)\n\nSince the function approaches the line $y=4$ as $x\\to \\infty ,$ we conclude that the equation of the horizontal asymptote is $y=4.$",
